* sg13cmos5l-bandgap -- closed-loop startup bench body (issue #30, Part of #5).
* Template: sim/grid-run.sh substitutes @@VDD@@ (the ramp target) and @@WORK@@ and writes the
* deck to sim/build/closed-loop-startup/deck.cir. The model `.lib` cards and the temperature
* come from the `klt sim` request (request-grid.json).
*
* This bench's own fixture note: the rail itself ramps 0 -> @@VDD@@ over 200 us and holds to
* 2 ms, so what the simulator solves for is the assembled block's own cold-start ->
* closed-loop-settled transition; the ramp and window are carried over unchanged from the
* inherited source bench, whose record is what the Startup row's self-starting claim cites.
* The ramp target below is the NOMINAL 3.30 V, not a placeholder for the corner's supply:
* `alter` cannot sweep a PWL source, so `corners.supply_waveforms: ["Vvdd"]` rescales the
* PWL levels per corner (peak -> the corner's supply) and klt reports what ran per point.
* The startup cell's release is graded on v(xdut.xx3.det) -- why, and what the source body's
* Vmkfb ammeter measured, is in README.md.
*
* The five explanations shared by every grid deck fixture (NO SPICE TITLE LINE,
* rshunt/gmin as convergence aids, the `.global sub!` substrate tie, the `Xdut`
* staged-DUT wrapper, osdi_preload instead of a .control block) are stated once in
* sim/README.md under "Deck fixture conventions", not repeated per template.

.options rshunt=1e9 gmin=1e-9

Vvdd vdd 0 PWL(0 0 200u @@VDD@@ 2m @@VDD@@)
Vvss vss 0 dc 0
.global sub!
Vsub sub! vss dc 0

.include "@@WORK@@/bandgap_top.spice"
Xdut vdd vref vss sg13cmos5l_bandgap_top
